Z||000000||Slammed Fiero||02-05-2001||01:34 AM||||I have a muffler from B&B triflow that was designed for a c2 porsche 911. According to my muffler guy it can be turned into a dual exhaust unit like the fiero's stock muffler quite easily. This exhaust is just sitting on my floor and is worth about $900 , all stainless , very nice piece of work. Is there anythiing to be gained by adding adding a performance exhaust. The body of the system is like the one on the fiero's , very short.<P>What are the advantages and disadvantages of doing this??<P>Thanks<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Slammed Fiero||02-05-2001||01:41 AM||||My motor is basically bone stock with the exception of a cold air intake and no cat converter..<P>Thanks<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||02-05-2001||07:59 AM||||I know your planning on doing a turbo sooner or later. My system came with its own exhaust system from manifolds back. The exhaust is designed to provide the right amounts of back pressure to make it work correctly. Too free of a flow like headers, etc wont allow the turbo to supply enought boost, unless the turbo itself is designed to work with it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||02-05-2001||08:23 AM||||going form a 1 7/8" cat and stock exhaust with a 2.5" cat and exhaust system with a little fuel and timing tuning i gained an ASTONISHING 51hp!!!!!!  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstdyno_manymods.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstdyno_manymods.jpg</A>  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/spiraldyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/spiraldyno.jpg</A>    <P>i think most of that was the TINY clogged cat and fuel delivery issues. but the larger exhaust will help a lot. my former Nitrous car was a MONSTER after the new exhaust... it was much more responsive under the fog and sounds just brutal... <P>ask GT Bastard if you'd like another testamonial... <P>I had a B&B on my 94 300ZX-TT. that was a piece of art work! all polished and not a single weld that wasn't PERFECT! it fit even better than the stock exhaust! <P><BR>and uum down from the turbo your exhaust can NEVER be to large. Bowling green makes a 5" down pipe for a Grand National!!! that will support well over 2000hp! more than any GN i've ever seen and they are installed on MANY street cars I've seen and straped down on the dyno! the design one turbo system would benifit from a set of sprint manifolds.. the others you'd have to manipulate and ruin to make work with a turbo unit.  <BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 02-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||88/2.8||02-05-2001||10:57 AM||||so, if you get rid of the cat. and put the right type of muffler, you will not loose any torque???||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||02-05-2001||02:12 PM||||CORRECT. i ditched the cat in my 86 SE and lost 4lbft of torque and only gain like 1.3hp.  the 2.8 aswell as most other motors need a good balance of back pressure and flow for the best torque and HP numbers.. the trick is finding that sweet spot. ||||reg||1
